Laryngitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Acute hoarseness/aphonia, Dry tickling cough, Erythematous/edematous vocal cords

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-024
Reason for Visit: Complete loss of voice and severe, painful coughing fits causing extreme distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, complete loss of voice (aphonia) and severe, harsh coughing in an adult following a cold. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute epiglottitis (a rapid, fatal airway emergency) or severe bacterial tracheitis, ensuring airway patency and providing immediate symptomatic management to prevent nocturnal airway compromise.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "(Written on paper) I cannot speak at all. My throat is raw, and I can't stop this dry, barking cough."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute, complete temporary voice loss (aphonia) developing over the last 12 hours. Endorses a severe tickling, scratchy, and raw sensation strictly localized to the anterior neck and larynx. Describes a dry, harsh, irritating, and non-productive cough. Notes frequent throat clearing provoked by the sensation of secretions. Antecedent symptoms of a mild viral upper respiratory tract infection 3 days prior. Reports mild discomfort when swallowing solid foods but denies severe odynophagia or shortness of breath.
Functional Status: Independent, communicating entirely via writing/text.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the inability to speak or call for help.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 85 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Comfortable at rest, visibly frustrated, communicating via notepad. No stridor, no drooling, no tripod positioning.
Physical Exam: Oropharynx is mildly erythematous but clear; uvula midline, no tonsillar exudate. Neck palpation reveals mild tenderness over the thyroid cartilage and larynx. Trachea is midline. Cervical lymph nodes are small and mobile. Chest auscultation is completely clear bilaterally with equal air entry and no wheeze. Attempted phonation produces only a strained, breathy whisper. (Note: Flexible laryngoscopy not available in home setting, but clinical presentation strongly suggests erythematous/edematous vocal cords).
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty lozenge packets; dry ambient air.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Viral Laryngitis - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Acute Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, absence of drooling, absence of severe odynophagia, no stridor, and the patient is comfortable supine without tripod positioning.

Vocal Cord Nodule/Polyp - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, sudden onset (12 hours) following a viral prodrome, rather than a chronic, progressive hoarseness over months related to voice abuse.

Laryngeal Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute onset, presence of viral symptoms, and lack of chronic progressive symptoms, weight loss, or massive fixed lymphadenopathy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe aphonia and a harsh cough, raising the immediate need to exclude a critical upper airway obstruction such as epiglottitis. Urgent physical examination confirmed a patent airway, normal oxygenation, and an absence of the classic "toxic" signs of epiglottitis (drooling, stridor, tripod posture). The clinical picture confidently pointed to severe acute viral laryngitis. By physically clearing the airway of immediate threat, the visit relieved severe patient anxiety and prevented an unnecessary emergency department transfer, allowing for safe, conservative home management centered on strict vocal rest.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised absolute, strict vocal rest (no whispering, which strains cords more than normal speech). Prescribed simple linctus (glycerol-based syrup) to soothe the dry cough. Advised liberal fluid intake.
Positive Behavioral Change: To enforce vocal rest and prevent permanent cord damage, the patient must strictly use a physical notepad and pen for all communication, refusing to even attempt a whisper. The immediate cessation of physical strain rapidly reduces the raw, scratchy laryngeal pain (positive reinforcement), proving the efficacy of rest and ensuring compliance with the silence mandate until the swelling subsides.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a cool-mist humidifier for the bedroom.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks if the voice has not fully returned, to consider ENT referral for laryngoscopy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops noisy breathing (stridor), severe difficulty breathing, sudden inability to swallow their own saliva, or a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; prolonged hoarseness requires specialist scoping.
Clinical Course: The complete loss of voice is temporary. With strict silence, the vocal cords will slowly un-swell over 7-10 days, and normal voice will return.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you start making a harsh whistling sound when you breathe in, or if you suddenly cannot swallow your own spit, call an ambulance immediately as your airway may be swelling shut.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A virus has severely swollen your vocal cords so they cannot vibrate, causing the loss of voice. Whispering actually forces the swollen cords together harder than normal speaking. You must rest them completely.
Actionable Steps: Do not speak or whisper at all; use your notepad. Drink plenty of room-temperature water. Run a humidifier in your bedroom tonight to soothe the dry cough.

